WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.300
What WilkinsonEyre are interested in,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.700
that the visitors to this gallery should get,

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:16.080
is the sheer wonder of actually, not all just the objects and the technology,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.595
but in a way the emotional connection;
the human experience of Medicine.

00:00:20.595 --> 00:00:22.780
And you get it all here.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.100
The first thing visitors will come

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:25.860
across is the three metre

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.900
high bronze statue by Marc Quinn;
fantastic focal point; creates the first

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.620
vista and can be seen kind of right from the
other side of the Eastern Hall, and this

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.200
really puts the visitor and human body
right at the front of the narrative.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.220
So we have, stripped back to the basic
architecture so things that in a sense

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.180
were superfluous and somehow didn't look
as if they belong;  we've taken a lot of

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.410
things out. But one of the other things
we've done is let the daylight in.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.690
So, you get these changes in pace from light
to dark;  you bring the outside world into

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.059
the space. I don't think it should
always be about sort of removing people

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:05.520
from reality and always be about a black box.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.940
One of the key things was to create these vistas

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.260
that would draw visitors through
the spaces. And we spent a lot of time

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.150
placing objects: open display, 
lighting them dramatically or having

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.750
mass displays to draw people across,  I
think that's particularly important when

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.960
you get to the void over Making the Modern World and you actually see

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:33.100
right across the gallery and are 
drawn in by large-scale AV or amazing objects.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.200
We're trying to turn in a sense what's three galleries into one gallery.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.700
So we're going through a
sequence of spaces and in the middle of

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.460
them was a big array of lifts and it was
fundamental as to how we could make

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.990
the two main parts of the gallery feel
connected and I think we've really

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.890
succeeded with that in a number of ways
that we've done it.  One is having a

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:00.030
unifying floor finish running through; 
this rather beautiful wooden floor,  and

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:06.150
the other is to actually cloak the lifts
in a whole range of objects.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.990
They act as partly to terminate vistas but
also to take you on to the next thing.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:16.240
So you feel there is a continuity
here;  which I think that at the outset

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.720
looked quite difficult. But we were
always thinking about it.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.320
With this being an object rich gallery, 
one of the challenges was how to display

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.260
the cases with the appropriate
environmental conditions that they all require.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.400
But also without creating a barrier between the visitor and the objects.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.970
The design of the display cases
have their technical challenges but

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:37.600
we've used non reflective glass,
practically frameless, and so you get

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.100
up close and personal with the objects.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.900
Another key element was communicating the themes to the visitor.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.900
We've got a lot of objects and a lot of different themes.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.100
So we worked very closely with the interpretation team and the graphic design team

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.790
and came up with the kind of
graphical hierarchy of totems which work

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:56.600
as signals as you go through the
galleries.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:04.690
Highlighting a particular section focus object or even
key objects within the narratives.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:08.200
At the far Western End of the gallery we have
another threshold space;  the look and the

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.700
feel of this space was in contrast of
the others spaces

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.209
as it's meant to feel quite intimate and a space where you can sit and

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:19.700
think about all the stories you've come
across and your journey through the other galleries.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.770
If we design this right and
we get this right in every way that we can,

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.200
the gallery will last 20 to 25
years.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.300
And that you'll come in in 20 years time and it will still look quite fresh.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.120
And if we can do that then I think we've really achieved something.

